      "text": "To solve the given equation \\(x^2 + y^2 = 10x - 6y - 34\\) for the value of \\(x + y\\), we will follow a step-by-step process of completing the square and simplifying the equation.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the given equation:\n\\[x^2 + y^2 = 10x - 6y - 34.\\]\n\nNext, we will move all terms to one side of the equation:\n\\[x^2 - 10x + y^2 + 6y = -34.\\]\n\nNow, we will complete the square for both \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).\n\nFor \\(x\\):\n\\[x^2 - 10x\\]\nTo complete the square, we take half of \\(-10\\), which is \\(-5\\), and square it to get \\(25\\). So, we add and subtract \\(25\\):\n\\[x^2 - 10x = (x - 5)^2 - 25.\\]\n\nFor \\(y\\):\n\\[y^2 + 6y\\]\nTo complete the square, we take half of \\(6\\), which is \\(3\\), and square it to get \\(9\\). So, we add and subtract \\(9\\):\n\\[y^2 + 6y = (y + 3)^2 - 9.\\]\n\nNow, substitute these completed squares back into the equation:\n\\[(x - 5)^2 - 25 + (y + 3)^2 - 9 = -34.\\]\n\nCombine the constants on the left side:\n\\[(x - 5)^2 + (y + 3)^2 - 34 = -34.\\]\n\nAdd \\(34\\) to both sides of the equation:\n\\[(x - 5)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = 0.\\]\n\nSince the sum of two squares is zero, each square must be zero:\n\\[(x - 5)^2 = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad (y + 3)^2 = 0.\\]\n\nThis gives us:\n\\[x - 5 = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x = 5,\\]\n\\[y + 3 = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad y = -3.\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(x + y\\) is:\n\\[x + y = 5 + (-3) = 2.\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\\boxed{2}.\\]",
