    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(x^2 + y^2 = 10x - 6y - 34\\), we start by rearranging it into a more familiar form. We do this by moving all terms to one side of the equation:\n\n\\[x^2 + y^2 - 10x + 6y + 34 = 0.\\]\n\nNext, we complete the square for both the \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) terms. Starting with the \\(x\\) terms, we have:\n\n\\[x^2 - 10x = (x - 5)^2 - 25.\\]\n\nFor the \\(y\\) terms, we have:\n\n\\[y^2 + 6y = (y + 3)^2 - 9.\\]\n\nSubstituting these completed squares into the equation, we get:\n\n\\[(x - 5)^2 - 25 + (y + 3)^2 - 9 + 34 = 0.\\]\n\nSimplifying the constants on the left side, we have:\n\n\\[(x - 5)^2 + (y + 3)^2 - 25 - 9 + 34 = 0,\\]\n\\[(x - 5)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = 0.\\]\n\nSince the sum of two squares is zero, each square must be zero. Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[(x - 5)^2 = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad (y + 3)^2 = 0.\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\n\\[x - 5 = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y + 3 = 0,\\]\n\\[x = 5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y = -3.\\]\n\nThus, the sum \\(x + y\\) is:\n\n\\[x + y = 5 + (-3) = 2.\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{2}.\\]",
